def add(key,title,body,image,role,refs,cite):
 assert len(body)==3
 new[key]={'title':title,'body':body,'transcript':body[:],'layout':'figure-right','figure':image,'figure_width':5.7,'instructional_role':role,'refs':refs,'cite':cite,'writing_level':'Concrete teaching; 5–7 rendered lines per paragraph.'}
add('galvani','Electrical signals take time to travel through nerves',[
'**Luigi Galvani** stimulated a frog nerve with electricity and observed the attached muscle contract. The response remained possible in isolated nerve and muscle tissue. A nerve contains axons, the outgoing processes of neurons. Electrical stimulation could therefore recruit a muscle response without requiring the animal’s whole brain to remain attached.',
'**Hermann von Helmholtz** measured the delay between nerve stimulation and muscle contraction. Stimulating at two positions along the same nerve changed the distance the signal traveled. Dividing that extra distance by the extra delay estimated conduction speed while using the same muscle response at the end of the pathway.',
'Helmholtz measured neural conduction at approximately **27.4 m/s**. This was far slower than electrical transmission along a wire. A neural signal travels as successive regions of membrane change their ion permeability and voltage. Its speed depends on properties of the axon, including diameter and the presence of an insulating myelin sheath.'
],oldfig(64),'foundation',[BOOK],'Carlson & Birkett (2022)')
add('myelin','Myelin affects signal timing and motor learning',[
'**Myelin** is an insulating sheath around an axon; gaps called **nodes of Ranvier** contain channels that regenerate the action potential. Comparing example speeds of 10 m/s and 150 m/s, a signal traveling one metre would take 100 ms or about 6.7 ms. Faster conduction changes when an input reaches its target.',
'Xiao and colleagues examined **oligodendrocytes**, the cells that make central nervous system myelin, while adult mice learned to run on a wheel with unevenly spaced rungs. Newly forming oligodendrocytes increased within 2.5 hours in underlying white matter and within four hours in motor cortex. The cellular response began early in the training period.',
'Deleting **Myrf**, a gene needed for oligodendrocyte development, in precursor cells prevented production of new oligodendrocytes. Running performance then diverged from comparison mice within two to four hours. Learning the new movement required more than executing existing muscle commands: the cells responsible for making myelin also changed during experience.'
],fig('myelin_learning','Xiao et al. (2016), Fig. 1A–C. Complex-wheel running and the requirement for new oligodendrocytes.','myelin'),'research',[BOOK,REF['myelin']],'Xiao et al. (2016); Carlson & Birkett (2022)')
new['myelin']['refs'].append('The 10 m/s and 150 m/s comparison speeds were supplied by the instructor. The 100 ms and 6.7 ms transit times are distance/speed calculations, not measurements reported by Xiao et al. Myelin-dependent conduction is explained in Carlson and Birkett, pp. 53–54.')
add('muller','A signal’s pathway determines its sensory meaning',[
'**Johannes Müller’s doctrine of specific nerve energies** states that the sensory experience depends on which pathway carries activity. Action potentials share the same basic electrical mechanism across sensory nerves. Activity arriving through an optic pathway is interpreted as visual input, whereas activity arriving through an auditory pathway is interpreted as sound.',
'The triggering event and the resulting experience can therefore be different. Mechanical or electrical activation of a visual pathway can produce a light-like sensation even without light entering the eye. The brain receives activity from the visual channel; the identity of that channel helps determine how the activity is processed.',
'A sensory nerve delivers its signals to particular brain circuits. Those circuits process the input and influence the actions appropriate to it. Activating selected sensory neurons can test this organization experimentally: the evoked response depends on the targeted cells and their outputs, rather than on electricity or light having one universal behavioral effect.'
],oldfig(12),'foundation',[BOOK],'Carlson & Birkett (2022)')
add('flourens','Removing brain tissue reveals functions that are lost',[
'**Pierre Flourens** used **experimental ablation**, removal of selected brain tissue, to examine nervous system function. He compared what an animal could do before and after an operation. Changes in purposeful movement and sensory reflexes provided observable outcomes through which he investigated the contribution of the removed brain region.',
'The method begins with a specific change to anatomy and measures a specific change in behavior. Removing tissue can interrupt cells within the region and signals traveling through it. Describing exactly which action fails is therefore more informative than describing the animal as generally impaired after the operation.',
'Broca applied the same structure–function reasoning to people whose brains had already been damaged. He examined speech and later inspected the injury; he did not experimentally remove human brain tissue. Modern clinical studies also examine the anatomy remaining after surgery and measure how behavior and brain activity are organized afterward.'
],oldfig(16),'foundation',[BOOK]+base['slides'][16-2]['refs'],'Carlson & Birkett (2022); Kliemann et al. (2019)')
add('purkinje','Purkinje identified distinctive cells in brain and heart',[
'**Jan Purkinje** described large neurons in the **cerebellum**, a brain structure important for coordinating movement. A Purkinje neuron has an extensive tree of dendrites that receives many synaptic inputs. Its axon provides inhibitory output to other cerebellar cells, so changes in its firing alter the activity sent through movement-control circuits.',
'**Cardiac Purkinje fibers** are a different cell type: specialized heart muscle cells that rapidly conduct excitation through the ventricles. Their electrical activity helps coordinate contraction of the heart muscle. They are not cerebellar neurons. The shared name records Purkinje’s anatomical discoveries, while the cells perform different jobs in different organs.',
'Cell shape makes a useful anatomical starting point. In the human cerebellar recording, fluorescent dye reveals the cell body and its branching dendritic tree, extending toward a millimetre across the tissue. Identifying the cell allows its structure, recorded electrical responses, and position in a circuit to be examined together.'
],fig('purkinje_cell','Masoli et al. (2024), Fig. 1A. Dye-filled human Purkinje neuron; original 100 µm scale bar.','purkinje'),'foundation',[BOOK,REF['purkinje']],'Carlson & Birkett (2022); Masoli et al. (2024)')
new['purkinje']['refs'].append('Anatomical correction: cardiac Purkinje fibers are specialized cardiomyocytes, not neurons. This distinction corrects the imprecise description in the supplied Introduction text; the visible teaching states the cell identities directly.')
add('atlas','Cell identity can be measured from gene expression',[
'Golgi’s stain and Cajal’s microscopy made individual neurons and their branches visible, work recognized by the **1906 Nobel Prize**. Yao and colleagues extended cell identification by measuring **RNA**, molecular copies of gene instructions, in individual mouse brain cells. Different patterns of gene expression distinguished populations with different cellular properties.',
'The study profiled approximately seven million cells, with about four million passing quality filtering. Its classification contained 34 broad classes and 5,322 smaller clusters. A cluster groups cells with similar expression patterns. The investigators also measured RNA at known positions in brain sections, retaining information about where those cells were located.',
'Cells with similar molecular identities often occupied characteristic brain regions. Measuring both identity and position therefore gives more information than counting neurons alone. A recognizable cell shape, a set of expressed genes, and a location describe different features of the same tissue, each useful for identifying which cells a circuit contains.'
],fig('atlas_types','Yao et al. (2023), Fig. 1B–E. Cell populations grouped by molecular identity, brain region, and transmitter type.','atlas'),'research',[BOOK,REF['atlas']],'Yao et al. (2023)')
table={'kind':'textbook','path':'figures/nobel_table.png','caption':'Carlson and Birkett (2022), Table 1.1, p. 22. Eight neuroscience-related awards, 1906–2017.','source_url':'https://books.google.com/books?vid=ISBN9781292430256','isbn':'9781292430256','pdf_page':23,'printed_page':22,'figure_id':'Table 1.1'}
add('nobel_table','Nobel discoveries explain different steps in neural function',[
'The **1906 award** recognized nervous system structure; the **1936 award** recognized chemical transmission. A neuron is a distinct cell, and a signal can pass between cells through a released chemical messenger. These discoveries identify both the anatomical units and a mechanism through which one cell changes the activity of another.',
'The **1963 award** concerned ionic mechanisms of nerve membranes, and the **1970 award** concerned neurotransmitters. Channels change membrane voltage; released transmitters act on receiving cells. The **1981 award** included visual processing and hemispheric specialization. These levels of explanation distinguish electrical events, chemical communication, and the operations of organized brain systems.',
'The **2000 award** concerned neural communication and plasticity, the **2014 award** spatial positioning, and the **2017 award** molecular circadian clocks. Experience can change synaptic responses; neurons can represent location; clock proteins can organize daily activity. Each discovery identifies a process that can be measured, altered, and examined through behavior.'
],table,'foundation',[BOOK],'Carlson & Birkett (2022)')
add('acetylcholine','Chemical release can be measured as a changing signal',[
'Dale and Loewi’s work, recognized in **1936**, established chemical transmission between cells. **Acetylcholine**, or ACh, is one such neurotransmitter. A nerve ending releases the molecule, and it binds to receptors on a receiving cell. Jing and colleagues made a fluorescent sensor that becomes brighter when ACh binds to it.',
'The sensor was expressed in mouse brain tissue. Electrical stimulation then recruited release from local nerve endings, producing a fluorescence change. In a tested protocol, stimulation ran at 100 Hz for five seconds. The signal rose after stimulation and declined afterward, providing a record of chemical availability over time.',
'The researchers also detected ACh signals in flies during odor presentation and body stimulation, and in mice during behavior. A transmitter response can therefore be timed relative to a sensory event or action. The measurement adds a chemical signal to the electrical spike and movement measurements used to explain nervous system function.'
],fig('acetylcholine','Jing et al. (2020), Fig. 2A–J. Acetylcholine signals evoked by electrical and sensory stimulation.','ach'),'research',[BOOK,REF['ach']],'Jing et al. (2020)')
# Retain the already verified original Nagel figure for a distinct explanation of the voltage-clamp experiment.
NAGEL=next(r for r in base['slides'][41-2]['refs'] if '1936192100' in r)
add('ionic','Holding voltage reveals current through an ion channel',[
'The **1963 Nobel Prize** recognized Eccles, Hodgkin, and Huxley’s work on ionic mechanisms of nerve membranes. A **voltage clamp** holds membrane voltage at a chosen value while measuring current. This separates the investigator’s chosen voltage from the ions moving through the channels, allowing channel behavior to be examined directly.',
'Nagel and colleagues expressed channelrhodopsin-2 in frog **oocytes**, immature egg cells, and delivered blue light near 450 nm. Illumination produced a current through the introduced protein. Changing the held voltage changed the current’s magnitude and direction, showing how the same open channel behaves under different electrical conditions.',
'Changing the available ions also changed the response. Positive ions could pass through ChR2, so opening the channel supplied a route for charge to cross the membrane. This experiment applies the ionic explanation of excitation to a light-sensitive protein: illumination changes permeability, and the resulting current can change a neuron’s voltage.'
],oldfig(42),'research',[BOOK,NAGEL],'Nagel et al. (2003); Carlson & Birkett (2022)')
add('fritsch','Stimulating cortex recruits movements across the body',[
'In **1870, Gustav Fritsch and Eduard Hitzig** applied weak electrical current to the exposed cortex of dogs. Stimulating particular sites caused particular muscles to contract. The movement occurred on the opposite side of the body, demonstrating **contralateral control**, in which a brain region influences actions on the other side.',
'The experiment changed neural activity and measured a motor output. Different stimulated sites recruited different movements rather than one uniform response to current. Cortical signals travel through descending pathways toward circuits controlling muscles. Identifying the stimulated location and the resulting contraction gave researchers a way to investigate this organization.',
'Light-sensitive proteins provide another way to activate neural tissue. The selected cells must express the protein, and illumination then changes their membrane current. Comparing stimulation with movement preserves the experimental question: which action can activity in the targeted circuit recruit? Optical targeting adds cell selection and precise stimulus timing to that question.'
],oldfig(12),'foundation',[BOOK],'Carlson & Birkett (2022)')
photo={'kind':'web','path':'figures/laureates_2026.jpg','caption':'Photo: Nature (2026). Karl Deisseroth, Peter Hegemann, and Georg Nagel, left to right.','source_url':'https://www.nature.com/articles/d41586-026-03185-x','credit':'Stanford Medicine; Humboldt-Universität zu Berlin/Philipp Plum; Robert Emmerich/Universität Würzburg, as credited by Nature.','license':'Published portraits; photographer/institution rights retained. No separate open license stated.'}
BOYDEN=next(r for r in base['slides'][44-2]['refs'] if 'nn1525' in r)
add('laureates','Optogenetics earned the 2026 medicine Nobel',[
'**Peter Hegemann** investigated how light affects algae that swim toward illumination. With collaborators, he identified channelrhodopsins as proteins that make a membrane responsive to light. A light-absorbing molecule changes the protein’s state, opening an ion pathway. This explained how an optical signal could directly produce an electrical current in a cell.',
'**Georg Nagel** and collaborators characterized these proteins with electrophysiology, including expression of ChR2 in frog egg cells. Blue light opened a channel that carried positive ions; changing voltage and ionic solutions changed the recorded current. The experiments established a usable light-gated conductance, providing the biophysical tool required for optical control.',
'**Karl Deisseroth** helped develop neuronal applications of the tool. In Boyden and colleagues’ 2005 study, cultured neurons expressed ChR2 and fired action potentials after brief blue-light pulses. This enabled experiments that select neurons, time their activation, and measure the resulting response. Nature reported the three laureates’ 2026 medicine Nobel on 5 October.'
],photo,'research',[REF['award'],NAGEL,BOYDEN],'Graham (2026); Nagel et al. (2003); Boyden et al. (2005)')
new['laureates']['figures']=[new['laureates'].pop('figure'),oldfig(44)];new['laureates']['layout']='figures-right';new['laureates']['primary_figure_height']=2.2
new['laureates']['refs'].append('Award verified from the accessible Nature report, 5 October 2026. The official Nobel press-release endpoint returned HTTP 403 and was not read. Hegemann and Nagel’s contributions were collaborative; Boyden and colleagues’ neuronal demonstration included Deisseroth rather than being a sole-author invention.')
add('dopamine','A timed dopamine signal changes reward learning',[
'The **1970 Nobel Prize** recognized discoveries concerning chemical transmitters, including their formation, storage, and release. **Dopamine** is a transmitter that helps regulate learning and action. Steinberg and colleagues tested how changing the activity of dopamine neurons at a particular moment affected a rat’s later response to a reward-predicting cue.',
'The investigators expressed ChR2 in targeted neurons and stimulated them when sucrose arrived. A one-second train contained five-millisecond light pulses at 20 Hz. The manipulation increased a neural signal at reward delivery, when the animal could learn whether a newly presented cue predicted an outcome beyond what it already expected.',
'Rats receiving the timed activation later responded more strongly to the new cue. **Prediction error** is the difference between an expected outcome and the outcome obtained. The experiment tested the hypothesis that dopamine activity at this moment supplies a teaching signal, allowing experience to change which cues subsequently recruit reward-seeking behavior.'
],fig('dopamine_learning','Steinberg et al. (2013), Fig. 2A–F. Dopamine-neuron activation at reward delivery promotes cue learning.','dopamine'),'research',[BOOK,REF['dopamine']],'Steinberg et al. (2013)')
add('visual','Visual experience changes the balance between the eyes',[
'Hubel and Wiesel’s visual-system work was recognized in **1981**, alongside Sperry’s work on hemispheric specialization. Visual circuits change with experience. **Ocular dominance** describes the relative contribution of the two eyes to visual processing. Steinwurzel and colleagues measured this balance in normally sighted adults before and after altered visual experience.',
'Participants built towers from wooden blocks while viewing their actions through a camera-and-headset system. The image reaching one eye was delayed by **333 ms** during a **one-hour** session. Afterward, that eye gained relative dominance. Delaying the same image during passive viewing did not produce the same change.',
'Replacing one eye’s image with a uniform gray display also changed dominance. The active delay condition produced a shift of similar size without removing the visual scene. Coordinating an action with temporally mismatched input therefore altered the balance of visual processing, extending the study of experience-dependent visual function to an adult sensorimotor task.'
],fig('visual_actions','Steinwurzel et al. (2025), Figs. 1A–C and 2. Visuomotor delay and short-term ocular-dominance changes.','visual'),'research',[BOOK,REF['visual']],'Steinwurzel et al. (2025)')
add('memory','Making proteins changes the strength of a synapse',[
'Kandel’s memory research was recognized in the **2000 Nobel Prize**, shared with Carlsson and Greengard. In the sea slug **Aplysia**, researchers can culture a sensory neuron with a motor neuron and measure the receiver’s voltage response. A larger **excitatory postsynaptic potential**, or EPSP, indicates a stronger excitatory response at that synapse.',
'Lee and colleagues used repeated **serotonin**, or 5-HT, treatment to induce **long-term facilitation**, a lasting increase in synaptic response. Five treatment pulses increased the EPSP measured 24 hours later. Reducing the protein **ApCPEB4**, which helps regulate production of other proteins from RNA, blocked the induction of this longer-lasting change.',
'Increasing ApCPEB4 made one serotonin treatment sufficient to induce facilitation. The protein therefore changed how much stimulation was required to produce a persistent synaptic response. Experience can alter communication between existing cells through molecular changes, providing a cellular mechanism through which earlier events can affect a later action.'
],fig('synaptic_memory','Lee et al. (2016), Fig. 4A–D. ApCPEB4 regulates induction of 24-hour synaptic facilitation.','memory'),'research',[BOOK,REF['memory']],'Lee et al. (2016)')
add('clock','Clock proteins organize daily activity through a small circuit',[
'**Circadian rhythms** are approximately 24-hour cycles generated within an organism. Hall, Rosbash, and Young received the **2017 Nobel Prize** for molecular clock mechanisms. In flies, the **period** gene produces PER protein, which participates in feedback that regulates the daily cycle of gene activity and helps organize the timing of behavior.',
'Reinhard and colleagues restored a functioning period gene in only **two DN1a clock neurons per hemisphere** of otherwise clock-deficient flies. DN1a neurons are an identified dorsal brain cell group. Locomotor recordings showed recovery of the basal daily activity pattern even though most of the adult clock network lacked a functioning molecular clock.',
'The researchers compared activity under light–dark cycles and constant darkness, including recordings at 20 °C and 25 °C. The traces describe when flies moved across successive days. A molecular oscillation in identified cells could therefore organize a whole-animal activity pattern, making the path from gene function to neuronal timing to behavior measurable.'
],fig('clock_behavior','Reinhard et al. (2025), Fig. 2A–L. Restricted period rescue and daily locomotor patterns.','clock'),'research',[BOOK,REF['clock']],'Reinhard et al. (2025)')
